    "shop_method": [
      "Ten-second test: lift the wheel, spin it and squeeze the lever gently; a squeal that pulses once a turn points to the rotor, a steady squeal to the pads",
      "Tools: isopropyl alcohol, a clean rag, the key for the caliper bolts (usually a 5 mm hex or a T25 Torx) and fine sandpaper",
      "Clean the rotor with isopropyl alcohol on a rag, twice, and keep fingers off the braking surface",
      "Rub glazed or lightly contaminated pads in a figure of eight on fine sandpaper until the shine is gone; replace soaked pads",
      "Align the caliper by loosening both bolts, holding the lever hard, and tightening the bolts in turns",
      "Pads glaze and squeal when they were never bedded in or got hot on a long descent; bedding in lays an even layer of pad material on the rotor",
      "Bed in with 10 stops from jogging to walking pace, then 5 harder stops, never holding the brake at a standstill, because that can leave a pad print on a hot rotor"
    ],
